problems.problem:
Let $\dagger$ be an operation on the set of nonnegative integers with the following properties: for all nonnegative integers $x$ and $y$,

(i) $(x+1) \dagger 0 = (0 \dagger x) + 1$;

(ii) $0 \dagger (y+1) = (y \dagger 0) + 1$;

(iii) $(x+1) \dagger (y+1) = (x \dagger y) + 1$.

If $123 \dagger 456 = 789$, find $246 \dagger 135$.

The operations below can be applied to any expression of the form $ax^2 + bx + c$:

(I) if $c \ne 0$, replace $a$ by $4a - \dfrac{3}{c}$ and $c$ by $\dfrac{c}{4}$;

(II) if $a \ne 0$, replace $a$ by $-\dfrac{a}{2}$ and $c$ by $-2c + \dfrac{3}{a}$;

(III$_t$) replace $x$ by $x - t$, where $t$ is an integer (different values of $t$ may be used).

Is it possible to transform $x^2 - x - 6$ into each of the following by applying some sequence of the above operations?

(a) $5x^2 + 5x - 1$ $\qquad$ (b) $x^2 + 6x + 2$

Let $n$ be a positive integer. An $n \times n$ matrix is called platinum if:

(i) its $n^2$ entries are integers from $1$ to $n$;

(ii) each row, each column and the main diagonal (from the upper left to the lower right corner) contains each integer from $1$ to $n$ exactly once;

(iii) there is a collection of $n$ entries containing each of the numbers from $1$ to $n$, no two in the same row or column, and none of them on the main diagonal.

Determine all values of $n$ for which an $n \times n$ platinum matrix exists.

Let $\mathbb{N}_0$ be the set of nonnegative integers. Find all nondecreasing functions $f : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{N}_0$ such that
$$f(x) = f\left(\frac{x}{n}\right) + f\left(\frac{x+1}{n}\right) + f\left(\frac{x+2}{n}\right) + \cdots + f\left(\frac{x+n-1}{n}\right)$$
for all positive reals $x$ and positive integers $n$. (A function $f$ is nondecreasing if $f(x) \le f(y)$ whenever $x \le y$.)
